{"type": "thinking", "text": "Budget sheet: Spent $67, Available 33%, Total $100. Correct.\n\nNow, the Biweekly calculated $58.33 — is that right after spending 67? It's the current biweek's allowance computed at biweek start ($100 for 24 days × 14 = 58.33). Hmm, but \"Calculated amount\" should maybe reflect the amount available now for the current biweek? The breakdown showed \"Spread over what's left of the period: $100/24 × 14 = 58.33\" (current biweek's fixed allowance) and \"For next biweek $34\". The home banner shows \"For next biweek $34\" (remaining now). So the sheet's \"Calculated amount\" is the current biweek's allowance, not remaining. OK, it's a display choice. Hmm, but with Daily, the sheet's calculated amount was $1.9 (today's allowance at day start), while available day remaining is... consistent.\n\nWait, actually there might be an inconsistency: after spending 67, the current biweek allowance computed at biweek start should be $58.33 (start of biweek = period start, zero spent). That's \"biweekly amount\" for the CURRENT biweek. But the home pill/banner shows \"For next biweek $34\" — for the NEXT biweek. The sheet's \"Calculated amount\" might be expected to show $34 (current remaining) instead of $58.33? Eh.\n\nLet me examine the \"Calculated amount\" label more: \"Calculated amount $58.33 Tap for the full breakdown\". With Daily it was $1.9 = today's allowance, and the breakdown's second part \"For tomorrow $1.7\". The home banner uses \"For tomorrow $1.7\". So the sheet's main figure = current period segment allowance; consistent across daily/biweekly. Fine.\n\nNow, I should test \"Weekly\" too - calculated amount should be $29.17. Already saw. OK.\n\nNext: history lazy sections. Let me create more dated expenses. I'll use the current Coffee $1 (today) — move it to 10 Nov. And the Unnamed $5 — keep today. Then add 2 new $2 expenses and move to Nov 15, 16? Hmm.\n\nActually, to make the history scroll, we need enough content. Currently:\n- Today expanded: $5 + $1 (rows ~168 each) \n- 13 Nov: 2 items (40,20)\n- 12 Nov: Coffee 1\nIf all expanded, total height: header 400 + today(2×170+70) + 13(2×170+70) + 12(170+70) + subscriptions ~ 1600. Screen 2400 minus nav... It nearly fit (last obs bottom 1363). Add 2 more sections ($1 each) → ~1900, might scroll a bit.\n\nBut to trigger lazy building, maybe scroll enough to recycle. With sections, LazyColumn lazily composes items as needed. The bug might be: section totals wrong after scrolling (e.g., sticky header duplicates). Let me create 4 more expenses on different dates to get 7 sections ≈ needs scrolling.\n\nPlan: For each new expense:\n1. Main: tap 2, tap check → saves Unnamed $2 today.\n2. History: today section expand (maybe last state collapsed?) → tap the new expense → Edit → Change date → pick date → Accept → checkmark.\nThat's ~10 calls each. 4 expenses = 40 calls. Hmm, heavy but doable. Alternatively, I can edit one existing $1 Coffee to a date, and the $5 Unnamed to another date, but that just moves existing sections (still 3 sections + maybe 2 more from today's items?).\n\nHmm: Today currently has 2 items. If I move Unnamed $5 to Nov 10 and Coffee $1 to Nov 11, then sections: Today(empty? removed), 13 Nov(60), 12 Nov(?), 11 Nov($1), 10 Nov($5) → 4-5 sections. That's without creating new expenses! Then I can restore dates after. But moving the $5 and $1 requires editing each: 5 expense edits (40+20 move? no). Actually:\n- Unnamed $5 (today) → move to Nov 10.\n- Coffee $1 (today) → move to Nov 11.\n- Coffee $1 (12 Nov) → move to Nov 15.\nThat gives sections: Nov 15 ($1 Coffee), Nov 13 ($60), Nov 11 ($1), Nov 10 ($5) = 4 sections. Plus maybe Today empty disappears. That's 4 sections; still maybe not scrolling much.\n\nHmm, 4 sections × ~240 = ~960 + header 400 = 1360; not scrollable. To force scrolling need ~6+ sections.\n\nWe could also not expand sections (rows shorter), so less height. Scrolling requires expanded items.\n\nAlternative: create many expenses today (quick: tap 2, check; repeat 5 times) → Today section with 7 items when expanded = tall → scroll within today section. That would exercise lazy item building but not per-date sections. The changed feature is per-date sections; still, scrolling the list would build sections lazily if implemented that way (each section is an item). With 7 items in Today only, only one section.\n\nHmm. Maybe I'm overthinking; the harness likely injected a visible bug in one area. Let me look for other bugs first, then decide.\n\nLet me look again at the \"daily amount exceeded\" banner. There's a subtle thing: after saving $5 at 08:55, the banner showed \"Daily Amount exceeded / For tomorrow $1.75\" — correct. But the pill (with no input) normally shows \"Today $1.9\" when not exceeded. After exceeding, it shows the banner instead of \"Today $X\"? Hmm.\n\nWait — the top pill when no spend shows \"Today $1.9\". After spending 5, it shows \"Daily Amount exceeded / For tomorrow $1.75\". So the pill transforms into a warning. That's intended.\n\nAnother potential bug: in the banner, for Biweekly it says \"For next biweek $33\" while the budget sheet said \"For next biweek $34\" earlier. Now after the extra $1 expense, remaining 33 → banner $33, correct.\n\nHmm OK.\n\nLet me test the recurrent payment toggle now: enter amount, tap the calendar icon (Recurrent payment), see mode. Then maybe save a recurring expense? The rent is recurring; adding another recurring expense would create scheduled repeats (data change, hard to revert). Better toggling and cancelling.\n\nTest:\n1. Enter 3.\n2. Tap recurrent icon (element 2 at 964,149).\n3. Observe change (maybe sheet shows \"Recurrent Expense\" toggle on, or date/time).\n4. Cancel by clearing.\n\nLet's do."}
